#### Article 8

1. Social protection for women and children is a joint obligation among the Regional Government, the community, social organizations, and the business sector, implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner.  
2. Social protection for women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) is prioritized for women and children who have lives that are not humanely decent and have the following social problem criteria: a. poverty b. neglect;  
   c. disability d. social dysfunction and behavioral deviation; and/or e. victims of gender-based violence and children and/or mistreatment, as well as victims of human trafficking;  
   f. victims of social conflict.  
3. Social protection for women and children, as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented through: a. prevention efforts;  
   b. handling efforts;  
   c. recovery efforts;  
   d. protection and support efforts.

#### Article 9

Prevention Efforts as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (3) letter a, can be carried out among others through efforts:  
 a. social counseling and guidance;  
 b. social assistance;  
 c. improvement of health and education levels;  
 d. improvement of access to resources;  
 e. empowerment.

#### Article 10

1. Handling Efforts as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (3) letter b, can be carried out among others through efforts: a. outreach (raid operations);  
   b. identification;  
   c. selection;  
   d. social motivation;  
   e. social guidance and assistance.  
2. In the framework of handling efforts as referred to in paragraph (1), further actions can be taken consisting of: a. referral to Temporary Shelter House;  
   b. referral to Social Rehabilitation Institution/Center;  
   c. return to parents/guardian/family/place of origin;  
   d. provision of health services.

#### Article 11

1. Recovery Efforts as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (3) letter c, can be carried out among others through efforts: a. Initial motivation from outreach results (raid operations);  
   b. Identification;  
   c. Selection;  
   d. Social motivation;  
   e. Distribution/referral to rehabilitation centers;  
   f. Return to place of origin;  
   g. Social guidance and skills training;  
   h. Stimulant assistance;  
   i. Supervision.  
2. Handling of rehabilitative efforts can be carried out in centers within the region and outside the region.  
3. In the implementation of rehabilitative efforts as referred to in paragraph (1), can be provided through guidance, education, training both physical, mental, social, medical rehabilitation, work skills according to their talents and abilities, social assistance, distribution and follow-up development.

#### Article 12

Protection and support efforts as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (3) letter d, can be carried out through efforts to provide facilities which include:

a. Child Care Social Institution that can receive referrals from private institutions and is equipped with workshop facilities;  
b. Women's Work Center;  
c. Provision of facilities/accessibility for persons with disabilities in buildings and public facilities.

#### Article 13

Technical implementation of social protection and the administration of social welfare for women and children as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (2) is further regulated by a Regent Regulation.

## Part Two

Social Protection and Administration of Social Welfare for Women

#### Article 14

Every Woman has the right to obtain social protection in the form of equal treatment and opportunity in education and training, employment, and protection of safety and/or health relating to women's reproductive function in accordance with norms of decent living and livelihood.

#### Article 15

Every woman is obliged to maintain her dignity and that of her family as well as her nature as a woman by taking into account her function and role in family and/or community life in accordance with her abilities.

#### Article 16

1. The Regional Government and/or the community provides social protection and the administration of social welfare to women who are unable, neglected, exploited, trafficked, and prostitutes.  
2. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (1), through: a. education and training services;  
   b. opportunities to work and do business;  
   c. physical, religious, mental, and social guidance;  
   d. health services;  
   e. legal assistance;  
   f. other special social protection.  
3. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (2) is the responsibility of SKPD/UKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.  
4. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for women as referred to in paragraph (2), can be organized by the community, social organizations, and the business sector.

#### Article 17

Every institution, both government and private, that organizes public services or employs women must provide adequate facilities for the interests of women.

## Part Three

Social Protection and Administration of Social Welfare for Children

#### Article 18

1. Every child has the right to receive social protection and the administration of social welfare from parents, family, the community, and the regional government to grow and develop normally.  
2. Every child has the right to receive social protection and the administration of social welfare to develop their abilities and social life in accordance with the culture and personality of the nation to become good and useful members of society.  
3. Every child has the right to receive maintenance of the child's welfare level and protection from environments that endanger and/or hinder their growth and development normally.  
4. Every child has the right to receive first aid, assistance and protection in dangerous situations. Every child has the right to receive protection from parents against all forms of physical and mental violence, neglect, mistreatment, exploitation and sexual abuse, as well as the right to care, religious guidance, and mental and social guidance.

#### Article 19

1. Everyone is prohibited from neglecting, committing acts of violence and/or exploitation against children.  
2. Everyone is prohibited from employing children under working age as regulated in the provisions of applicable laws and regulations.

#### Article 20

The regional government and/or the community can carry out social protection and the administration of social welfare for children for the purpose of improving child welfare, carried out in accordance with statutory provisions.

#### Article 21

1. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for children as referred to in Article 18, in the form of: a. Care and custody b. Health services and nutritional improvement;  
   c. Education and recreation services;  
   d. Religious, mental and social guidance;  
   e. Social rehabilitation;  
   f. Social assistance;  
   g. Family reunification;  
   h. Population administration and civil registration services;  
   i. Funeral services j. Legal assistance services;  
   k. Maintenance of social welfare level l. Provision of child care facilities at the workplace m. Other special social protection.  
2. For children with disabilities, in addition to receiving social protection and the administration of social welfare for children as referred to in paragraph (1), also receive accessibility services.  
3. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for children as referred to in paragraph (1), is the responsibility of SKPD/UKPD in accordance with their duties and functions.  
4. Social protection and the administration of social welfare for children as referred to in paragraph (1) can be organized by the community, social organizations and the business sector.  
5. Further provisions regarding forms of social protection and the administration of social welfare for children as referred to in paragraph (1), are regulated by a Regent Regulation.
